\title{Survival Multiplicities in Vertex-Deleted Graph Decks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a finite simple graph, its vertex deck is the multiset of induced subgraphs obtained by deleting one vertex at a time, with cards considered up to isomorphism. The Reconstruction Conjecture asserts that every finite simple graph of order at least three is determined up to isomorphism by this deck. The conjecture is open in general. We develop a self-contained counting framework for information visible across the deck. We prove a uniform-family double-counting identity, derive Kelly's counting lemma for induced subgraphs, specialize it to an exact edge-sum identity, and show that edge count is reconstructible from any deck of order at least three. Consequently, edgeless and complete graphs of order at least three are reconstructible. We also describe direct algorithms and examples. The unifying principle is that a feature supported on exactly $k$ vertices survives in exactly $n-k$ one-vertex-deleted observations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A graph may be observed indirectly through a collection of damaged copies. Given a finite simple graph $G$, delete each vertex in turn together with all incident edges. Forget the names of the remaining vertices and retain the resulting collection of isomorphism types. The central question is whether this collection determines $G$.

The Reconstruction Conjecture states that every finite simple graph with at least three vertices is determined up to isomorphism by its vertex deck. We do not claim a proof of that conjecture. Instead, we establish the counting core that explains why many numerical invariants can be read from a deck and prove reconstruction at the two extremal edge densities.

The central mechanism is double counting. If an object uses $k$ vertices of an $n$-vertex ambient set, then it survives deletion of exactly the $n-k$ vertices outside its support. Summed over all deletion cards, every such object is counted with multiplicity $n-k$. Applied to induced copies of a fixed graph $F$, this gives Kelly's counting lemma. Applied to a single edge, it shows that the sum of all card-edge counts is $(n-2)$ times the original number of edges.

The restriction $n\geq3$ is essential for edge recovery: only then is $n-2$ positive and cancellable. For a two-vertex graph, every vertex-deleted card is a one-vertex graph, whether the original pair was adjacent or not.

\section{Graphs, cards, and survival}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{finite simple graph} is a pair $G=(V,E)$ where $V$ is finite and
\[
E\subseteq\{\{x,y\}:x,y\in V,\ x\neq y\}.
\]
Its order is $|V|$ and its size is $|E|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Graphs $G=(V,E)$ and $H=(W,D)$ are \emph{isomorphic}, written $G\cong H$, if there is a bijection $\varphi:V\to W$ such that
\[
\{x,y\}\in E\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\{\varphi(x),\varphi(y)\}\in D
\]
for all distinct $x,y\in V$.
\end{definition}

For $S\subseteq V$, the induced subgraph $G[S]$ has vertex set $S$ and all edges of $G$ with both endpoints in $S$.

\begin{definition}
For $v\in V$, the \emph{vertex-deleted card} is $G-v=G[V\setminus\{v\}]$. The \emph{vertex deck} is the multiset
\[
\mathcal D(G)=\{[G-v]:v\in V\},
\]
where brackets denote isomorphism class and multiplicity is retained.
\end{definition}

We say that $G$ and $H$ have the same paired deck if there is a bijection $\psi:V(G)\to V(H)$ for which $G-v\cong H-\psi(v)$ for every $v$. For finite decks, this expresses multiset equality together with a chosen matching. A graph of order at least three is reconstructible if every graph with the same deck is isomorphic to it.

\begin{definition}
Let $V$ be an $n$-element set. A family $\mathcal A$ of subsets of $V$ is $k$-uniform if $|S|=k$ for every $S\in\mathcal A$. For $v\in V$, its surviving subfamily is
\[
\mathcal A_v=\{S\in\mathcal A:v\notin S\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{The uniform-family identity}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform-family double counting]
Let $V$ have $n$ elements and let $\mathcal A$ be a finite $k$-uniform family of subsets of $V$. Then
\[
\sum_{v\in V}|\mathcal A_v|=(n-k)|\mathcal A|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Count
\[
\Omega=\{(v,S)\in V\times\mathcal A:v\notin S\}.
\]
Choosing $v$ first gives $|\Omega|=\sum_v|\mathcal A_v|$. Choosing $S$ first, each $k$-set excludes exactly $n-k$ vertices, so $|\Omega|=(n-k)|\mathcal A|$. Equating the two counts proves the formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
If $k=n$, no member survives any deletion and both sides vanish. If $k<n$, inversion gives
\[
|\mathcal A|=\frac{1}{n-k}\sum_{v\in V}|\mathcal A_v|.
\]
Thus divisibility by $n-k$ is structural.
\end{remark}

\section{Induced patterns and Kelly's lemma}

Let $F$ be a graph on $k$ vertices and $G$ a graph on $n$ vertices. Define
\[
\mathcal I_F(G)=\{S\subseteq V(G):|S|=k\text{ and }G[S]\cong F\},
\qquad N_F(G)=|\mathcal I_F(G)|.
\]
Copies are counted by support sets, not by the number of isomorphisms onto a support.

\begin{theorem}[Kelly's counting lemma]
For every finite simple graph $G$ on $n$ vertices and finite simple pattern $F$ on $k$ vertices,
\[
\sum_{v\in V(G)}N_F(G-v)=(n-k)N_F(G).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The family $\mathcal I_F(G)$ is $k$-uniform. Its members surviving deletion of $v$ are precisely the supports of induced copies of $F$ in $G-v$. Apply the uniform-family theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recovery of smaller induced-pattern counts]
If $k<n$, then
\[
N_F(G)=\frac{1}{n-k}\sum_{v\in V(G)}N_F(G-v).
\]
Thus the count of every fixed induced pattern smaller than $G$ is determined by its counts across the deck.
\end{corollary}

Taking $F$ to be a triangle recovers the triangle count when $n>3$: every triangle survives in $n-3$ cards. At $k=n$, the visibility multiplier vanishes. This marks the distinction between counting proper substructures and reconstructing the whole graph.

\section{Edge reconstruction}

Let $K_2$ be a single edge. Since $N_{K_2}(G)=|E(G)|$, Kelly's lemma immediately gives the following identity.

\begin{theorem}[Vertex-card edge-sum identity]
If $G$ is a finite simple graph on $n$ vertices, then
\[
\sum_{v\in V(G)}|E(G-v)|=(n-2)|E(G)|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
An edge $e=\{x,y\}$ appears in $G-v$ exactly when $v$ is neither endpoint. There are $n-2$ choices of $v$. Thus summing edge counts over all cards counts each original edge exactly $n-2$ times.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Edge count from the deck]
For $n\geq3$,
\[
|E(G)|=\frac{\sum_{v\in V(G)}|E(G-v)|}{n-2}.
\]
In particular, the numerator is divisible by $n-2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}[Five-cycle]
For $C_5$, every deletion produces a four-vertex path with three edges. Hence
\[
\sum_v|E(C_5-v)|=5\cdot3=15=(5-2)\cdot5,
\]
and the formula returns five edges.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Five-vertex star]
For $K_{1,4}$, deleting the center leaves zero edges, while deleting any leaf leaves three. Thus
\[
0+4\cdot3=12=(5-2)\cdot4.
\]
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Failure at order two]
The edgeless graph on two vertices and $K_2$ have identical decks: every card is a single isolated vertex. Yet their edge counts differ. Here $n-2=0$, so the edge sum carries no information.
\end{example}

\begin{lemma}[Isomorphism preserves edge count]
If finite simple graphs $X$ and $Y$ are isomorphic, then $|E(X)|=|E(Y)|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
An isomorphism maps unordered adjacent pairs bijectively to unordered adjacent pairs; its inverse supplies the inverse edge map.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Edge-count reconstruction]
Let $G$ and $H$ have the same paired vertex deck. If $|V(G)|\geq3$, then
\[
|E(G)|=|E(H)|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The deck pairing implies that both graphs have the same order $n$. Corresponding cards are isomorphic and have equal edge counts, so
\[
\sum_{v\in V(G)}|E(G-v)|=\sum_{w\in V(H)}|E(H-w)|.
\]
The edge-sum identity changes this to
\[
(n-2)|E(G)|=(n-2)|E(H)|.
\]
Because $n\geq3$, cancellation proves the result.
\end{proof}

Only the multiset of card-edge counts is used here, not the full adjacency data in the cards.

\section{Extremal reconstruction}

For fixed order $n$, a simple graph has between $0$ and $\binom n2$ edges. At either endpoint there is a unique isomorphism type.

\begin{theorem}[Reconstruction of edgeless graphs]
Every finite edgeless graph on at least three vertices is reconstructible from its vertex deck.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be edgeless and let $H$ have the same deck. Edge-count reconstruction gives $|E(H)|=|E(G)|=0$, so $H$ is edgeless. The deck pairing gives equal order. Any bijection between two edgeless graphs of equal order is an isomorphism.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reconstruction of complete graphs]
Every finite complete graph on at least three vertices is reconstructible from its vertex deck.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $G=K_n$ and let $H$ have the same deck. They have equal order, and edge reconstruction gives
\[
|E(H)|=|E(G)|=\binom n2.
\]
There are only $\binom n2$ unordered pairs of distinct vertices. Since $H$ contains the maximum possible number of edges, every pair is adjacent, so $H$ is complete and hence isomorphic to $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recognition by card-edge totals]
For $n\geq3$, a deck comes from an edgeless graph exactly when its total card-edge count is $0$, and it comes from a complete graph exactly when that total is
\[
(n-2)\binom n2.
\]
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

Represent a graph on $0,\ldots,n-1$ by normalized pairs $(u,v)$ with $u<v$.

\subsection{Deck construction}
For every vertex $v$, scan all $m$ edges and retain those not incident with $v$. Explicitly constructing all labeled cards takes $O(nm)$ time and can require $\Theta(nm)$ output space. Producing unlabeled isomorphism classes additionally requires canonical labeling or graph-isomorphism tests; numerical edge reconstruction does not require that step.

\subsection{Edge recovery}
Given card-edge counts $c_1,\ldots,c_n$ with $n\geq3$, form $S=\sum_i c_i$, verify divisibility by $n-2$, and return $S/(n-2)$. This uses $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. For a genuine deck divisibility follows from the edge-sum theorem. Failure certifies inconsistent input, although passing is not sufficient for deck realizability.

\subsection{Induced-pattern counting}
For a pattern $F$ on $k$ vertices, enumerate all $\binom nk$ supports, form each induced graph, and test isomorphism with $F$. A brute-force test over all $k!$ bijections checking $O(k^2)$ potential adjacencies gives total time
\[
O\!\left(\binom nk k!k^2\right).
\]
For fixed $k$ this is polynomial in $n$. Kelly's identity can be demonstrated by repeating the count in every card and comparing the sum with $(n-k)N_F(G)$.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

In a communication network observed under every possible single-station failure, a link remains visible unless one endpoint fails. It therefore appears in exactly $n-2$ observations. A motif using $k$ stations appears in exactly $n-k$ observations. This calibrates motif frequencies under systematic leave-one-out sampling.

Graphs also encode molecular skeletons, dependencies, and pairwise relations. Vertex deletion models removing one atom, component, or entity. Exact induced-pattern counts across fragments reveal counts in the original whenever the pattern is smaller than the whole graph.

The deck's redundancy is information rather than waste. Its overlapping observations resemble leave-one-out methods in statistics and inverse problems: predictable visibility multiplicity allows exact global totals to be recovered from partial views.

\section{Limitations and future directions}

These identities do not settle the Reconstruction Conjecture. Pattern counts lose location and overlap information. Cards are individually unlabeled, so there is no common coordinate system across observations. Kelly's inversion applies only to patterns with fewer than $n$ vertices, and equal edge counts are far weaker than isomorphism except at the extremal values.

A natural next target is the degree multiset. Since
\[
|E(G-v)|=|E(G)|-\deg_G(v),
\]
recovering $|E(G)|$ suggests recovering all degrees from the multiset of card-edge counts. This supports the study of regular graphs. Trees invite structural arguments through leaves, branches, and card component decompositions. One may also formulate induced-pattern reconstruction directly: equal decks should force equal counts $N_F(G)=N_F(H)$ whenever $|V(F)|<|V(G)|$.

Complements provide a further duality because deletion commutes with complementation:
\[
\overline{G-v}\cong\overline G-v.
\]
Thus reconstruction results for a class can potentially be transferred to its complementary class. The edgeless and complete theorems already form such a pair.

\section{Conclusion}

Every object supported on $k$ vertices survives exactly $n-k$ one-vertex deletions. The uniform-family identity turns this into a general counting theorem, and Kelly's lemma applies it to induced graph patterns. For edges,
\[
\sum_v|E(G-v)|=(n-2)|E(G)|.
\]
Consequently, equal decks of order at least three force equal edge counts. At the minimum and maximum possible edge counts this invariant determines the entire graph, proving reconstruction for edgeless and complete graphs.

The general conjecture remains open because totals do not encode the geometry of overlap. Nevertheless, survival multiplicity identifies exact information in a deck, explains the threshold of three vertices, and provides a foundation for stronger structural reconstruction arguments.

